    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Nineteenth-century US patent law drew a clear distinction between human innovations, which were patentable, versus products of nature, which were not. My thesis explores the transformation of nineteenth-century prairies into domesticated, commercial farmlands in the context of patent law. The trajectories of two intersecting phenomena in Marion County, Kansas, from 1860 to 1890 offer illuminating perspectives. One, the unpatented Turkey Red wheat brought to Kansas by the Russian Mennonites in 1874, was a prodigious wheat varietal from the Russian steppes, which quickly dominated the domesticated prairies of the Great Plains. The other phenomenon, the patented, revolutionary McCormick Reaper, could harvest vast lands with great efficiency. Geography, climate, and commercial forces brought patented mechanical innovation and un-patented nature into an abrupt symbiotic relationship, and forever transformed the Great Plains.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Vander Ploeg, M. (2024). PATENTS AND POWER: TURKEY RED WHEAT AND THE MCCORMICK REAPER; SMALL SEEDS, BIG INNOVATIONS, AND THE TRANSFORMATION OF PRAIRIES 1860 to 1890. Version 1. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/qp883zz6677/version/1. https://doi.org/10.25740/qp883zz6677.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
